questions.answer:
const storage = {
  setJSON(key, value) {
    try {
      localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(value));
      return true;
    } catch (err) {
      console.error("storage write failed", err.name);
      return false;
    }
  },
  getJSON(key, fallback = null) {
    try {
      const raw = localStorage.getItem(key);
      return raw === null ? fallback : JSON.parse(raw);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error("storage read failed", err.name);
      return fallback;
    }
  },
};

storage.setJSON("user", { name: "Kakade" });
console.log(storage.getJSON("user").name);
console.log(storage.getJSON("missing", { name: "guest" }).name);
// Kakade
// guest

Both methods catch because localStorage can throw SecurityError (storage disabled) and QuotaExceededError, and JSON.parse throws SyntaxError on corrupt text left by another version of the app. setJSON returns a boolean so callers can degrade gracefully instead of crashing. getJSON distinguishes a missing key (null) from stored JSON null, which JSON.parse would also return. Neither method stores the raw object, so no type information is lost silently beyond what JSON already loses.

