questions.answer:
const text = JSON.stringify({
  id: 7,
  createdAt: new Date("2026-03-01T10:00:00Z"),
  name: "Kakade",
});

const revive = (key, value) =>
  key.endsWith("At") && typeof value === "string" ? new Date(value) : value;

const obj = JSON.parse(text, revive);
console.log(obj.createdAt instanceof Date, obj.createdAt.getUTCFullYear());
// true 2026

text is {"id":7,"createdAt":"2026-03-01T10:00:00.000Z","name":"Kakade"}. The reviver runs for every key, bottom-up, and its return value replaces that property. The typeof check prevents double-converting an already-parsed value and avoids treating a non-date string as a date. key.endsWith("At") is a naming convention, not proof: validate with Number.isNaN(date.getTime()) before trusting it. Returning undefined from a reviver deletes the key. A replacer can perform the reverse transform, but it cannot make JSON preserve a Date type by itself.

